.TH AUSHA 1 "2026-09-20" "ausha @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ausha \- stream desktop audio to receivers on the local network
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ausha
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ausha
captures whatever this machine is already playing and sends it as Opus audio in
RTP to receivers on the same network: the Ausha Android app, or
.BR ausha-recv (1)
on another computer.
.PP
On start it prints a pairing token and a QR code and then waits. Receivers
register over a TCP control channel and receive media over UDP. The token
changes on every start unless
.B \-\-token
pins it.
.PP
With
.B \-\-uplink
the traffic also runs the other way: one receiver's microphone is published on
this machine as a capture device named
.BR ausha ,
so a call taken here can be spoken into from the phone.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-control\-port " port"
TCP control channel port. Default 6996.
.TP
.BI \-\-media\-port " port"
UDP media port receivers punch and listen on. Default 6997.
.TP
.BI \-\-bitrate " kbps"
Opus bitrate. Default 128. Lower it on a struggling network.
.TP
.BI \-\-token " token"
Use a fixed pairing token instead of generating one.
.TP
.BI \-\-static\-client " addr"
Always send media to this
.IR ip:port ,
with no handshake.
.TP
.BI \-\-sdp\-out " path"
Write an SDP describing the
.B \-\-static\-client
stream, playable with
.BR ffplay .
.TP
.BI \-\-compat\-ts " ip:port"
Also push MPEG-TS to this address, for mpv or VLC. Repeatable. This skips the
handshake, so it skips pairing and is not covered by
.BR \-\-encrypt :
use it only on a network you trust.
.TP
.BI \-\-capture " device"
Capture this device instead of the detected one.
.TP
.B \-\-encrypt
Encrypt the media payload with ChaCha20-Poly1305, keyed by the pairing token.
Ausha receivers follow automatically;
.B ffplay
and
.B \-\-sdp\-out
cannot.
.TP
.B \-\-uplink
Accept a receiver's microphone and publish it as a capture device named
.BR ausha .
One receiver at a time. Linux only.
.TP
.BI \-\-uplink\-port " port"
UDP port the microphone arrives on. Default 6998.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-speakers
Keep playing through this machine's speakers while receivers are connected,
instead of muting them. Muting is Linux only.
.TP
.BI \-\-name " name"
Name advertised to receivers. Defaults to this host's name.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-discovery
Do not advertise over mDNS. Receivers must then be pointed at this machine by
address.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-qr
Do not print the pairing QR code.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a usage summary and exit.
.SH NETWORK PORTS
TCP 6996 for control, UDP 6997 for media and UDP 6998 for the uplink, all
configurable above. A firewall that drops these is the usual reason a receiver
times out. Many guest and hotel networks block device-to-device traffic
entirely, which Ausha cannot work around.
.SH EXAMPLES
Stream to a phone or another computer:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ausha
.EE
.RE
.PP
Take a call on this machine and speak into it from the phone:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ausha \-\-uplink
.EE
.RE
.PP
Then choose
.B ausha
as the microphone in the call application.
.PP
Reuse one token so receivers do not have to re-pair on every restart, and
encrypt the audio:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ausha \-\-token my\-secret \-\-encrypt
.EE
.RE
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.BR ffmpeg (1)
built with libopus, for capture and encoding;
.BR pactl (1)
from pulseaudio-utils, to find the monitor source and to publish the uplink
device; and
.BR ip (8)
from iproute2, for mDNS advertisement.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ausha-recv (1),
.BR ffmpeg (1),
.BR pactl (1)
.PP
Full documentation at
.UR https://github.com/aaxis-em/ausha
.UE .
.SH BUGS
Report at
.UR https://github.com/aaxis-em/ausha/issues
.UE .
